winewayland: Don't force a tiled configure size on fixed-size windows.

A tiled configure is only a size suggestion, but wayland_configure_window
applied it like a maximized one. A window without a sizing border can't take
it: the application sizes such windows itself, and games do so on every
screen-mode change. Elden Ring in windowed mode under niri went into a fight
in which every round applied the tile size, the game re-applied its own,
the window grew by two pixels, and the swapchain was recreated: 831
SetWindowPos calls and 524 swapchain re-creations in 38 seconds, with the
window ending up taller than the screen.

Treat a tiled-only state on a non-resizeable window as carrying no size, the
same as the stateless case. The compositor centers the window in its tile,
which is what every other fixed-size Wayland client gets.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -549,8 +549,15 @@
 
     state = surface->processing.state;
     /* Ignore size hints if we don't have a state that requires strict
-     * size adherence, in order to avoid spurious resizes. */
-    if (state)
+     * size adherence, in order to avoid spurious resizes. A tiled configure
+     * is only a suggestion, and a window without a sizing border can't take
+     * it: the application sizes such windows itself (games do so on every
+     * mode change), so forcing the tile size starts a fight in which each
+     * round resizes the window and its swapchain. Leave those at their own
+     * size; the compositor centers them in the tile. */
+    if (state &&
+        !(!data->resizeable &&
+          !(state & ~(WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_TILED | WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_RESIZING))))
     {
         width = surface->processing.rect.right - surface->processing.rect.left;
         height = surface->processing.rect.bottom - surface->processing.rect.top;
